# Git Branch & Commit Strategy

Align branches and commits with the topic packages under `com.practice.*`
(`arrays`, `dp`, `graphs`, `trees`, …).

## Branches

- One problem (or tightly related variants) per branch.
- Name: `{package}/{problem-kebab-case}`
  - `{package}` = folder under `com.practice` (e.g. `graphs`, `arrays`)
  - `{problem-kebab-case}` = short problem slug from the class/problem name
- Examples:
  - `graphs/rotting-oranges`
  - `graphs/detect-cycles-in-2d-grid`
  - `arrays/two-sum`
- Do not use vague names (`fix`, `updates`, `wip`).
- Legacy underscore names like `graph_rotting_oranges` are historical; use the
  package/slash form going forward.

## Commits

- Prefer the topic prefix matching the package (capitalized):
  - `Graphs: add Detect Cycles in 2D Grid solution and tests`
  - `Arrays: add Two Sum brute-force and hashmap variants`
- For non-problem work:
  - `chore: …` (tooling, wrapper, CI)
  - `docs: …` (README, rules)
- Keep commits focused: solution, tests, and same-problem variants may share a
  commit; unrelated topics stay on other branches.
- Do not mix multiple unrelated problems in one commit.
- Run `./mvnw test` (or targeted `./mvnw -Dtest=…Test test`) before committing
  problem code.

## Workflow

1. Start from updated `main`.
2. `git checkout -b {package}/{problem-kebab-case}`
3. Implement under `src/main/java/com/practice/{package}/` and matching tests.
4. Commit with the topic-prefixed message.
5. Open a PR into `main` when ready (only if asked).
